# Delete merged feature branches

When a pull request for this repo is **merged** (or the user says the PR landed / shipped), delete the branch it was built on. Do not leave finished `cursor/` feature branches sitting around.

## Confirm first

1. Verify the PR is merged (`gh pr view <n> --json state,mergedAt` or the GitHub UI). Do not delete on open, draft, or closed-unmerged PRs.
2. Note the head branch name from the PR (e.g. `cursor/f9-command-registry-mvp-linear-msho-13`).

## What to delete

1. Check out `master` (or the repo default base) and pull so local trunk matches the merge.
2. Delete the **local** branch: `git branch -d <branch>` (use `-D` only if `-d` refuses after a confirmed merge and the tip is fully contained on trunk).
3. Delete the **remote** branch if it still exists: `git push origin --delete <branch>` (or `gh pr view`’s head ref cleanup). GitHub’s “Delete branch” after merge is equivalent; do it if the remote tip remains.
4. Prune stale remote-tracking refs: `git fetch --prune`.

## Never delete

- `master` / `main` / the default base branch
- A branch that still has an open PR
- A branch the user explicitly asked to keep
- Someone else’s branch you did not confirm was theirs / this PR’s head

## When this fires

- Immediately after you (or the user) merge a feature PR
- When the user says a PR merged and asks to clean up
- During post-merge cleanup next to archiving finished OpenSpec changes

Default to deleting both local and remote once merge is confirmed. If only one side remains, delete that side.
